```python
def lcs_length(x, y):
    m, n = len(x), len(y)
    L = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]
    for i in range(1, m + 1):
        for j in range(1, n + 1):
            if x[i - 1] == y[j - 1]:
                L[i][j] = L[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
            else:
                L[i][j] = max(L[i - 1][j], L[i][j - 1])
    return L[m][n]
```

Each of the $(m+1)(n+1)$ entries is computed once, in constant time, from entries already filled in, so the running time is $O(mn)$. The last test uses strings of length $140$ and $120$: the plain recursion would never finish, but the table has only about $17\,000$ cells. Correctness is Theorem 6 (and the Lean theorems `lcs_optimal` and `lcs_achieved`).
